# The compose stack's own settings. compose interpolates this file into the
# stack files; a dev deployment also hands it to the services as their env
# file, while a prod deployment gives the services the file `KALLIPAI_ENV_FILE`
# names. Copy this file to `.env` in this same directory, then fill in what
# the deployment needs.
#
# The minimum: a dev stack needs KALLIPAI_DOMAIN alone. In dev, an edge on
# another host, off 443, or over http also sets KALLIPAI_EDGE_* and
# KALLIPAI_APP_ORIGIN. A prod deployment adds KALLIPAI_ENV_FILE, fills that
# file's credentials, and sets the prod keys at the end of this file.
#
# The tagma's settings live in the root `.env.example`: direnv exports them
# into the shell, and compose never reads them.

# Archeion / Relay (the platform services)
# Secrets below — populate compose/.env (gitignored), never commit. See
# docs/en/deployment/container.md for the per-mode contract.

# Dev domain for the Caddy-fronted topology (app and api subdomains).
# The code default is the prod domain (kallipai.com); local dev overrides it to a
# dev-only name so dev DNS/certs never clash with production. direnv reads
# compose/.env into the shell, so compose, mkcert and vite all see it. Prod
# ignores it (the prod stack takes real domains via the vars below).
# The key was called KALLIPAI_DEV_DOMAIN until 2026-08-27; nothing reads that
# name now, so a file copied before then needs this line renamed.
KALLIPAI_DOMAIN=kallipai.lan

# The compose stack (compose.yaml plus one override) derives the app origin
# from this domain, so CORS, the OAuth callback and the WebAuthn relying
# party move together. Two stacks on one host share the platform network by
# default and would answer to the same service names: give the second one
# KALLIPAI_PLATFORM_NETWORK=<other name>.
#KALLIPAI_PLATFORM_NETWORK=kallipai-platform

# The dev loop's edge knobs. KALLIPAI_EDGE_PORT (default 443) is the port the
# dev edge listens on: the compose stack writes it into the edge's site address
# (`*.<domain>:<port>`) and the API base. Compose derives the browser-facing
# origin as `https://app.<domain>` on 443, so an edge that answers under another
# host, off 443, or over http has to name KALLIPAI_APP_ORIGIN, which CORS, the
# OAuth callback and the WebAuthn origin compare verbatim. The web dev server
# reads EDGE_PORT as the HMR client port.
# KALLIPAI_EDGE_TLS=on (default) makes that HMR socket announce itself as wss,
# for the TLS edge in front; off announces ws, for a plain-http edge.
# KALLIPAI_EDGE_ENTRY names the entry the container runs: the dev override
# defaults to Caddyfile.vite against the mkcert pair, prod to the image's own
# Caddyfile against the host's pair. The plain entry mounts no certificate, and
# it is served from the three addresses below, which the dev edge passes through
# verbatim whenever they are set.
#KALLIPAI_EDGE_TLS=off
# Move the edge off 443 when the host's own server already holds it.
#KALLIPAI_EDGE_PORT=8443
#KALLIPAI_EDGE_ENTRY=/etc/caddy/Caddyfile.plain
#KALLIPAI_EDGE_SITE=http://app.localhost:8080
#KALLIPAI_API_BASE=http://api.localhost:8080
#KALLIPAI_MODEL_GW_SITE=http://model-gw.localhost:8080
#KALLIPAI_APP_ORIGIN=http://app.localhost:8080

# The admin token is a deployment value. The dev override pins the shared
# fixture (sk-admin-dev-..., so kallipai-admin logs in without reading logs)
# and wins over this file; a prod deployment sets a strong secret in its
# service env file (compose/.env.prod.example).

# The platform-internal secret is not set through the stack env file: the
# archeion provisions it itself (generated into its state directory on first
# boot, then only read) and the other services receive the file path.
# See docs/en/deployment/container.md for the deployment forms.
# OAuth sign-in providers (GitHub / Google). A provider's "Continue with X"
# button appears on the login + register pages only when BOTH its client id and
# secret are set (see archeion `configured()`). The archeion exchanges the
# authorization code server-side, so it must reach the provider's token endpoint
# over HTTPS (the dev container carries a CA bundle for this). Register the
# OAuth app at the provider with the callback URL
#   <KALLIPAI_ARCHEION_OAUTH_REDIRECT_BASE>/auth/callback
# -- the redirect base is the APP origin (the SPA owns the /auth/callback
# route that finishes the ceremony), NOT the archeion origin. Dev example:
# https://app.kallipai.lan/auth/callback  (app.<KALLIPAI_DOMAIN>). After
# setting these, restart the archeion (env_file is read at container start).
# Both stacks derive the redirect base from the app origin, so it is not set
# here. Dev reads the four provider keys below from this file; a prod
# deployment sets them in its service env file (compose/.env.prod.example).
#KALLIPAI_ARCHEION_OAUTH_GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=
#KALLIPAI_ARCHEION_OAUTH_GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET=
#KALLIPAI_ARCHEION_OAUTH_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
#KALLIPAI_ARCHEION_OAUTH_GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# The tagma's own auth values are not here: the operator token, the agent
# bearer and the relay enrollment code belong to the daemon and the
# operator-side CLIs, and they live in the root `.env.example`.

# --- archeion values the compose overrides decide -----------------------------

# Both overrides set these in service.environment, which wins over this file,
# so a line here would change nothing:
#   dev pins the store credentials, the database URLs, the cookie flag, the
#   trusted proxies and the admin token, and derives the CORS origins, the
#   session cookie domain and the WebAuthn relying party from KALLIPAI_DOMAIN,
#   taking KALLIPAI_APP_ORIGIN first for the origins when it is set; prod
#   derives the same browser-facing values from KALLIPAI_DOMAIN alone and pins
#   none of these.
# A prod deployment keeps these keys in the service env file named below;
# compose/.env.prod.example lists them.

# --- lesche (data-plane relay; separate process from the archeion)

# The listen address, the archeion internal URL and the CORS origins are compose
# values: compose.yaml pins the first two for both stacks, and each override
# derives the origins from KALLIPAI_DOMAIN; a dev stack takes
# KALLIPAI_APP_ORIGIN first when it is set, so a line here would change nothing.
# A lesche started outside compose reads the same variables from its own flags;
# the listen address defaults to 127.0.0.1:7200 and its unit supplies the rest.
# Postgres URL of the durable chat store (rooms, membership, message
# payloads). The dev override pins its own; a prod deployment sets this in its
# service env file (compose/.env.prod.example).

# --- files (content transfer; kallipai-files)
#
# The listen address, the archeion internal URL and the blob root are compose
# values: compose.yaml pins all three for both stacks and mounts the
# kallipai_files_blobs volume at the blob root, so a line here would change
# nothing. A files service started outside compose takes its variables from
# the table in docs/en/reference/environment-variables/files.md (the blob root
# and the database URL are required there); its archeion internal URL and token
# file are in docs/en/reference/files-api.md.
# The shared secret is the archeion-provisioned internal token; its file path
# is deployment configuration (KALLIPAI_POLIS_INTERNAL_TOKEN_FILE), not a .env
# value.
# Postgres URL of the metadata store. The dev override pins its own; a prod
# deployment sets this in its service env file (compose/.env.prod.example).

# --- prod stack only (compose.prod.yaml) -------------------------------------
# KALLIPAI_ENV_FILE is the environment file the services read at runtime:
# the deployed database credentials and tokens live there, not in this file.
# Give an absolute path. compose resolves a relative one against this
# directory while scripts/compose-up.sh refuses anything but absolute before
# a prod start, so a relative value hides a placeholder from one side or the
# other. The prod override refuses to render without the key; the dev
# override falls back to this file.
#KALLIPAI_ENV_FILE=/etc/kallipai/prod.env
#
# A prod start also needs GF_SECURITY_ADMIN_PASSWORD,
# KALLIPAI_INSTANCES_STATE_DIR and KALLIPAI_TLS_DIR: the prod override
# interpolates them and refuses to render while one is unset.
# scripts/compose-env.sh --example prod prints a value for each of them, and
# for KALLIPAI_DOMAIN and KALLIPAI_ENV_FILE.
#
# Both stacks interpolate TELEMETRY_BASE, the stack directory and the image
# tag. scripts/compose-up.sh supplies the stack directory and the image tag
# itself, and --defaults dev adds TELEMETRY_BASE and the daemon directories
# for a dev run (the dev override interpolates those two). A prod deployment
# sets TELEMETRY_BASE in this file: prod has no defaults file, and
# --example prod prints a value.
